<figure xml:id="interactive-upper-lower-sum">
    <caption>Experimenting with Approximations of Area</caption>
    <interactive platform="geogebra" width="100%" aspect="4:3" preview="images/RiemannLowerUpperCompare.png">
        <slate xml:id="geogebra-riemann-sums" surface="geogebra" material="rtqfummu" aspect="4:3">
                setCoordSystem(-0.5, 2.5, -0.4, 1.5);
                enableShiftDragZoom(true);
        </slate>
        <instructions>
            <p>Select various values of <m>n</m>. Compare the results of the approximations <mdash /> labeled lower sum and upper sum <mdash /> to the actual area.</p>
        </instructions>
    </interactive>
</figure>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-build-riemann-sum">
	<title>Constructing an Area Approximation</title>

	<activity xml:id="activity-build-riemann-sum">
		<title>Constructing an Area Approximation</title>
		<introduction><p>This activity provides directions for constructing an area approximation (called Riemann Sum)</p></introduction>
		<task><p>Sketch the curve <m>y=\sqrt{1-x^2}</m> on its whole domain <m>(-1,1)</m>.</p></task>
		<task><p>At each of the <m>x</m>-coordinates listed draw a vertical line. Each line should go from <m>y=0</m> to <m>y=1</m>. <m>x=-1,-\frac{3}{4},-\frac{1}{2},0,\frac{1}{4},\frac{1}{2},1.</m></p></task>
		<task><p>Notice that the vertical lines combined with the <m>x</m>-axis form three sides of a rectangle. For each of the <m>x</m> coordinates except <m>x=2</m> draw a top side on the rectangle starting at the height of the left segment. Notice that these are not all above and not all below the curve.</p></task>
		<task><p>Calculate the total area of the set of rectangles.</p></task>
	</activity>
</subsection>

</section>
